# MiniTool HQ — Customer-facing copy

All text that ships on the website (homepage, tool pages, FAQs, meta, CTAs, emails in UI) is for **customers** — sellers and creators using the tools.

## Do

- Speak to **their job**: listing photos, inbound cartons, uploads that fail, privacy of product photos
- Say what the tool **does for them** in plain English (size, pass/fail, download ZIP, no upload)
- Prefer concrete outcomes over brand philosophy
- Prefer **one tool per job** — if a seller uses Amazon and TikTok, they should not need two nearly identical pages

## Do not

- Paste **internal strategy** language onto the site (e.g. “job-first not toolbox-first”, “swiss army knife”, “vertical tools land over time”, “our wedge”, “marketplace later”, “we’re differentiating from TinyWow”)
- Mention **monetization** (ads, AdSense, Pro, $4.99, paywall, “how we keep the lights on”) — that stays in internal docs only; on-site just say free / no signup when relevant
- Write as if explaining the product to the **founder or the AI**
- Use meeting jargon, roadmap voice, or competitive-analysis tone in customer UI
- Split the **same job** across multiple tools just to chase keywords (cover long-tails inside one tool)

## Quick test

If a first-time visitor would ask “so what do I get?”, the copy failed. Rewrite for the visitor’s outcome.
